{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.lepton_tower_bend_invariant","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.lepton_tower_bend_invariant","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.bend_of_linear","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.bend_of_linear","kind":"theorem","line":146,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/lepton_tower_bend_invariant.lean#L146-L159","doc":"**A readout linear in the winding has bend `(w+2)²/(w(w+4))`.**\n\nAt the lepton windings `(3, 5, 7)` this is `25/21 ≈ 1.190`, independent of\nthe constant `c`.  Together with `bend_of_geometric` this covers the two\nfamilies every classical `T(2,n)` invariant falls into: the polynomial\nones (crossing number, determinant `= n`, genus, signature) land near\n`1.19`, the exponential ones land exactly on `1`, and the measured lepton\nbend is `12.295`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.geometric_readout_forces_unit_bend","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.geometric_readout_forces_unit_bend","kind":"theorem","line":124,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/lepton_tower_bend_invariant.lean#L124-L144","doc":"**The screening theorem.**\n\nA geometric readout forces `bend m = 1` for *every* `C`, `g` and `p` at\nonce.  Hence a tower with any measured bend other than `1` is incompatible\nwith the entire three-parameter class built on a geometric readout — no\nfine-tuning argument required, and no need to ask how large `p` would have\nto be.\n\nApplied to the leptons: the measured bend is `≈ 12.295`, so every\nexponential-type invariant of `T(2,n)` is excluded in one step. By\n`linFunctionalK_two_step` that includes every linear readout of `H₂(q)`\nup to its `O(q⁻ⁿ)` remainder — the Markov trace and the Jones polynomial\namong them. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.bend_of_geometric","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.bend_of_geometric","kind":"theorem","line":113,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/lepton_tower_bend_invariant.lean#L113-L122","doc":"**A readout that is exactly geometric in the winding has bend `1`.**\n\n`x(w) = a · r^w` gives `bend x w = 1` for every `w`: the numerator\n`a² r^(2w+4)` and the denominator `a² r^(2w+4)` agree identically. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.bend_of_scaled_geometric_pow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.bend_of_scaled_geometric_pow","kind":"theorem","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/lepton_tower_bend_invariant.lean#L89-L111","doc":"**The bend transports through the whole bridge class, and the two\nfitting knobs cancel.**\n\nIf `m_w = C · g^w · f(w)^p` then `bend m = (bend f)^p`: the overall scale\n`C` and the geometric factor `g` drop out *identically*, leaving the\nexponent as the only surviving freedom.\n\nThis is what makes the bend a screening tool rather than one more quantity\nto fit.  A candidate readout `f` is tested by comparing `bend f` against\nthe measured `bend m`, with two of the three free parameters already\neliminated. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.bend","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.bend","kind":"def","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/lepton_tower_bend_invariant.lean#L78-L87","doc":"The three-point **bend** of a tower at windings `w`, `w+2`, `w+4`:\n\n```\n  bend x w = x(w+2)² / (x(w) · x(w+4)).\n```\n\nMultiplicatively this is the log-concavity of the tower — `bend > 1` means\nthe log-increments are decreasing.  For the charged leptons at windings\n`(3, 5, 7)` it is `m_μ² / (m_e · m_τ) ≈ 12.295`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}